APP.PY --

from investment import get_company_data — this pulls in the function I already wrote and tested in investment.py.
This is why I built and tested it standalone first: now I can reuse it instead of rewriting it.

app = Flask(__name__) — creates actual web application object. Almost every Flask project starts this exact way.

@app.route("/") — this is called a decorator (the @ symbol). In English: "when someone visits the homepage URL (/), run the function right below me." 
This is the core magic of how Flask connects a URL to a piece of Python code.

render_template("home.html", company=company) — this tells Flask "go find an HTML file called home.html, and hand it the company data so the HTML can display it." 

app.run(debug=True) — starts the server. debug=True means if you make an error, 
Flask shows you a helpful error page in the browser instead of just crashing silently — very useful while learning.

Later updated to this->

from flask import Flask, render_template
from investment import get_company_data

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/")
def home():
    return render_template("home.html")

@app.route("/company/<ticker>")
def company_profile(ticker):
    company = get_company_data(ticker)
    return render_template("company.html", company=company)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(debug=True)



